nse":"The Jets defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 5th and 23rd. Will McDonald has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is David Bailey, who has 5.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a blitz rate that ranks 7th highest in the league.","special":"The Jets have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 87% of field goals, which is about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 8% of the money on the books. Their tight ends were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Geno Smith has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,623 yards. Garrett Wilson has been the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 444 receiving yards. Braelon Allen has handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, David Bailey leads the team with 5.5 sacks.","coaching":"Frank Reich is calling the offense and Aaron Glenn is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Browns under Kevin Stefanski and the 2024 Jaguars. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Buccaneers under Todd Bowles and the 2024 Patriots.","trends":"The offense went from 29th in EPA per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, a big step forward. The defense moved from 31st in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 15th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was pre-snap motion rate, which went from 63% to 49%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}